<!--
    GH-1034, P2 switch model, option switchrunonguard (default on), with
    switchrunon (default on).

    pick dispatches on a byte with no bounds check, the shape kuna-switchrunon
    covers, but first tests an unrelated parameter: test esi,esi; jne. The
    model records that branch as a guard although it says nothing about the
    byte, the way a Rust deserializer checks the Result tag of next_key()
    right before matching the key it holds. Its own table has four rows
    (cases returning 0x11..0x14); the 252 rows after them land in the bodies
    of the next two functions, left and right.

    Source (GNU as, linked at 0x100000): pick at 0x100000 (flag set returns
    0x99), left at 0x100100, right at 0x100200, the table at 0x100300 (4 own
    rows, then 126 pairs of rows into left and right).

    Pass 1 (default): the branch on flag does not narrow the byte, so the
        table counts as unguarded and stops at its four rows: pick prints its
        four cases, the flag branch, and none of left's or right's code (#1,
        #2, #6, and the single counts of #3-#5).
    Pass 2 (option switchrunonguard off): the recorded guard keeps the whole
        width, so left's and right's returns print as cases of pick up to
        0xff (#3-#5).

    pick2 (0x100800) sends selector 1 elsewhere before the dispatch
    (cmp eax,1; je), the way a Rust visitor loop skips one key, so its
    range runs 2..0xff and wraps to 0: the row of case 0 (returning 0x40)
    comes last, after the 252 rows into left2 and right2. Read in value
    order the table keeps cases 0, 2 and 3 (#7, #8, and the single counts
    of #9 and #10); with the option off it is read whole.
-->
